1
00:00:00,820 --> 00:00:08,790
On August 29, 1988, Joe Becker of Xerox proposed a common numbering system for characters across languages.

2
00:00:09,457 --> 00:00:14,587
Today, Unicode gives this Hangul letter Giyeok its own number.

3
00:00:15,198 --> 00:00:19,668
Let’s follow this letter back to Xerox in the years before that proposal.

4
00:00:20,822 --> 00:00:24,542
Back then, the same character could have a different number in each character table.

5
00:00:25,207 --> 00:00:31,637
The Xerox team compared Japanese and Chinese character tables to extend a Chinese font.

6
00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:40,290
Becker and Collins met Karen Smith-Yoshimura and Alan Tucker of the Research Libraries Group to discuss which Han characters to treat as the same.

7
00:00:40,947 --> 00:00:49,417
In July 1988 Apple bought the group's Chinese, Japanese and Korean character database to study Han unification.

8
00:00:50,082 --> 00:00:56,052
In 1988, Joe Becker proposed character codes that different systems could share.

9
00:00:56,698 --> 00:01:00,768
Now look at these two forms of the character for blue-green.

10
00:01:01,555 --> 00:01:04,675
Look at the lower halves. The strokes inside differ.

11
00:01:05,332 --> 00:01:08,412
The two characters had different codes in the Taiwanese standard.

12
00:01:09,082 --> 00:01:14,032
Give them one number and you lose track of which form the original document contained.

13
00:01:14,707 --> 00:01:18,887
That is why each kept its own Unicode code point.

14
00:01:20,030 --> 00:01:25,840
In March 1992, the joint group finalized the unified Han repertoire and its order.

15
00:01:26,498 --> 00:01:31,228
In the second volume, published that June, let’s open the cross-reference table to the character for “one.”

16
00:01:31,915 --> 00:01:36,275
In the Korean table, the same character is in row 76, column 73.

17
00:01:36,957 --> 00:01:40,787
The three table entries for “one” share a single Unicode code point.

18
00:01:41,457 --> 00:01:45,357
Convert it back to the Korean table, and it is row 76, column 73 again.

19
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:50,650
The aim was to share a character while preserving the distinctions in the original text.

20
00:01:51,790 --> 00:01:59,670
In February 2026 Lee Collins and two colleagues checked a dictionary for a character meaning “who” in the Tày language of Vietnam.

21
00:02:00,332 --> 00:02:04,062
The three proposed correcting the form of an existing character.

22
00:02:04,682 --> 00:02:09,822
Four decades after the Xerox project, Collins was still comparing written forms.

23
00:02:13,898 --> 00:02:21,498
Finally, Unicode 18.0, released in September 2026, encodes 172,808 characters.

24
00:02:22,165 --> 00:02:29,195
Its 175 scripts write thousands of languages, while its localization standard supports 172 base languages.

25
00:02:29,873 --> 00:02:37,633
Among living languages with their own encoded script, one of the rarest is Toto, spoken by about 1,500 people in one village in India.

26
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:49,460
At the other extreme, East Asian Han ideographs lead with 102,999 characters, while Korean Hangul leads single-language scripts with 11,739.

